# rule.require.generic-governs-structure-never-voice

> **telepath governs the STRUCTURE of a message. it governs the VOICE not at all.**

| axis | who owns it | examples |
|---|---|---|
| **structure** | 🔮 telepath | what the message says, in what order, in how many words, where each passage sits |
| **voice** | the adopter's mascot + the role that speaks | capitalization, emoji, persona, vibe phrases, mascot lines |

the two compose. a telepath rule fires inside an owl's voice here, a seaturtle's in `ehmpathy`, and any voice a future repo adopts — unchanged.

⇒ a structural rule is repo-agnostic by nature; a voice rule is repo-specific by nature. so **the seam is what makes the role reusable** — cross it, and a repo that wants the structure must take the foreign voice or fork the rule, and a fork drifts on the structural half.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you reach for an emoji in a telepath prescription | that is voice. state the structural property; let the adopter's mascot supply the glyph |
| you write *"say it in lowercase"* or *"open with the mascot"* | voice — it belongs in `im_a.<mascot>` |
| you write *"lead with the point, then the detail"* | structure — it is telepath's |
| an example needs a concrete message to show the shape | use this repo's voice — an example illustrates, it does not prescribe |
| a repo adopts a rule and its output looks wrong | check which axis broke. wrong order = telepath's defect. wrong glyph = the mascot's |

## .the test

> **would this rule read the same if the reader's mascot were a seaturtle instead of an owl?**

yes → structure. it is telepath's · no → voice. it belongs to whichever repo adopts it

## .examples — one rule, written three ways

| the specimen | the prescription | what an adopter can do with it |
|---|---|---|
| 👍 the bare rule | *"lead with the answer; the reason follows it"* | an owl obeys in lowercase with a 🦉, a seaturtle with a 🐢. the rule did not say, so both adopt it unchanged |
| 👎 the leaked rule | *"lead with the answer, in lowercase, prefixed with 🦉"* | `ehmpathy` cannot take it and keep its own mascot, so it forks |
| 👍 the demoed rule | *"an error names the fix, not merely the symptom"*, demoed as `🦉 bummer — no route bound. bind one with: rhx route.bind.set --route <path>` | the rule wears no voice and travels; only its example does |

⇒ **the demoed rule is the one to hold** — a concrete example is not a leak. an example illustrates, and only a prescription binds.

## 🟡 .the voice row is declared in ONE cell — cite it, never copy it

- a peer rule that restates the members will drift from them
  - the drift is silent — no reader ever holds two copies at once
  - ⇒ so a copy short one member reads as complete

⇒ **so every rule in this canon points at the table above rather than re-lists it.** measured, and two of the copies had already drifted — the full carries the walk.

## 🟡 .the owed migration

the output-half generics still live in `ehmpathy`'s mechanic + architect roles under `lang.prose/`, beside `lang.tones/`, that repo's voice cluster.

⇒ that co-location is this seam, drawn independently by another repo. when the move happens, `lang.prose/` comes and `lang.tones/` stays.

blocker: a telepath rule that prescribes a member of the voice row — it makes the rule unadoptable · a mascot brief that prescribes message order, length, or passage placement — it is structure, and it will drift from the generic that owns it · a peer rule that restates the voice row rather than cites it.
false positive: a telepath example written in the adopter's voice — an example must be concrete to illustrate at all.

⇒ see also: `define.bhrain-repo-mascot` (repo=.this) · `rule.require.place-a-passage-by-its-subject` (a structural rule that says not one word about sound) · `define.simplified-technical-english` (ehmpathy/mechanic).
